  BJT/FET/MOSFET + SCR/TRIAC\nETA/CETa SCOPE: 3.5–3.7.3\nCAREER/EMBEDDED 
 PURPOSE: Switching\, biasing\, logic-level MOSFETs\, flyback diodes\, Zene
 r regulation\, semiconductor fault symptoms.\n\nTHIS WEEK'S RESOURCES:\n-E
 lectronics for Beginners: https://www.youtube.com/playlist?list=PLzqS33DOP
 hJkRn6e9_OTdQwRojO8qlusI\n- NMOS vs PMOS / Enhancement vs Depletion: https
 ://www.youtube.com/watch?v=kY-ka0PriaE\n- ETA CETa competencies: https://w
 ww.etai.org/comps/CETa_comps.pdf\n\nMASTERY GATE: identify devices and ter
 minals\, analyze simple bias/current/power\, select a transistor/MOSFET as
  a switch\, and troubleshoot one intentionally miswired circuit.\n\nSYSTEM
  RULES: Watching alone never counts. Use learn -> retrieve -> calculate/co
 de -> build/measure -> debug ->document. Start each non-exam study block w
 ith ~10 minutes of spaced recall. Never use a missed session as justificat
 ion for an unhealthy make-up marathon.\n\nTODAY — ACTION CHECKLIST:\n1. 
 Open LAB-008 — Diode / BJT / MOSFET Switching in Practice → Lab Center
  → Week 08.\n2. Choose one route. Virtual needs: Web browser only. Physi
 cal needs: DMM diode mode\, LEDs/diodes\, BJT\, logic MOSFET\, resistors\,
  supply.\n3. Follow every numbered step under the route you selected\; pre
 dict the result before simulating\, building\, or measuring.\n4. Save the 
 required evidence: Device observations + BJT/MOSFET switch screenshots + f
 ault note.\n5. Mark only the route you actually completed. A virtual compl
 etion does not claim physical bench experience.\n\nREFERENCE HUB — KEEP 
 THESE LINKS:\n• Official Associate CET Study Guide\, 6th ed.: use your s
 canned/Google Drive copy and the PRINTED page numbers in BOOK ASSIGNMENT.\
 n• ETA 6th-ed. errata: https://www.etai.org/6thEdASTErrataSheet.pdf\n\nO
 FFICIAL STUDY GUIDE — V6 BOOK ASSIGNMENT\nREAD / USE: Ch.9 pp.70–78 + 
 Ch.10 pp.79–83.\nQUIZ / REVIEW: Ch.9 quiz p.78\, Ch.10 quiz p.83 and Uni
 t 4 review p.84.\nWHY: Directly supports semiconductor recognition\, switc
 hing behavior and discrete-device troubleshooting.\nSOURCE RULE: The 6th-e
 dition book is the primary CETa textbook. Current ETA competency wording +
  current ETA errata control whenever the older book differs or omits a cur
 rent topic.\n\nAFTER TODAY — REQUIRED LEARNING OUTCOMES (WEEK 08)\nBy th
 e end of this event\, you should be able to explain or demonstrate:\n• E
 xplain PN junction behavior and the operation/use of rectifier diodes\, LE
 Ds\, Zener diodes\, and protection diodes.\n• Explain BJT switching at a
  practical level and distinguish NPN/PNP behavior.\n• Explain FET/MOSFET
  gate control\, source/drain roles\, logic-level switching\, and common pr
 otection concerns.\n• Recognize SCR/TRIAC purpose and where controlled s
 witching devices are used.\n• Reason about diode drops\, current limitin
 g\, transistor/MOSFET switching conditions\, and device ratings.\n• Reco
 gnize whether a semiconductor device is being used safely within its state
 d limits.\n• Select and wire a basic transistor/MOSFET switch with appro
 priate current limiting/flyback protection.\n• Troubleshoot common semic
 onductor-switching faults with a meter or scope.\n\nCOVERAGE AUDIT\nThe as
 signed resources and tasks above are intended to cover every item in this 
 outcome list. If you still cannot explain or demonstrate one of these item
 s after the session\, do not count that item as complete. Write the concep
 t on a short catch-up list and use the linked official or supplemental res
 ource before closing the gap.\n\nWEEK-END MASTERY RULE\nThis practical is 
 the cumulative proof point for the week. The week is Green only when the k
 nowledge can be used on real hardware/code/measurements without following 
 a step-by-step tutorial.\n\nSIMPLE COMPLETION RULE\nFinish the assigned wo
 rk\, then check the learning outcomes below. If you cannot explain or demo
 nstrate an outcome\, write that one concept on a short catch-up list and r
 epair it during the next available review block. No spreadsheet logging is
  required.
