 ain\, power gain\, loading\, input/output impedance\, and feedback.\n• U
 se decibels at a basic technician level and relate d B changes to gain/los
 s.\n• Distinguish common amplifier classes and basic transistor/FET ampl
 ifier roles.\n• Explain ideal op-amp assumptions at an introductory leve
 l.\n• Calculate basic voltage/power gain and d B relationships.\n• Pre
 dict amplifier output limits and identify clipping/loading from numerical 
 or waveform evidence.\n• Recognize inverting\, non-inverting\, buffer/co
 mparator-style applications and the role of feedback.\n• Measure or simu
 late an amplifier and diagnose clipping\, bias\, loading\, or gain problem
 s.\n\nCOVERAGE AUDIT\nThe assigned resources and tasks above are intended 
 to cover every item in this outcome list. If you still cannot explain or d
 emonstrate one of these items after the session\, do not count that item a
 s complete. Write the concept on a short catch-up list and use the linked 
 official or supplemental resource before closing the gap.\n\nWEEK-END MAST
 ERY RULE\nThis practical is the cumulative proof point for the week. The w
 eek is Green only when the knowledge can be used on real hardware/code/mea
 surements without following a step-by-step tutorial.\n\nSIMPLE COMPLETION 
 RULE\nFinish the assigned work\, then check the learning outcomes below. I
 f you cannot explain or demonstrate an outcome\, write that one concept on
  a short catch-up list and repair it during the next available review bloc
 k. No spreadsheet logging is required.
