 o/alfred-university/resources.html\nCAREER CONNECTION: C + MCU bring-up + 
 serial buses + debug instrumentation.\nRESOURCE PRIORITY: CETa Core → Ca
 reer Core → Deep Dive. Complete the required work first\; use Deep Dive 
 for repair or extra depth.\n\nWEEK 16 FOCUS: HOLIDAY LIGHT: STM32 Setup + 
 GPIO + SWD Debug\nETA/CETa SCOPE: 13.12\; 15.3/15.6\; 12.1–12.3 reinforc
 ement\nCAREER/EMBEDDED PURPOSE: CubeIDE\, build/flash\, Cortex-M basics\, 
 GPIO\, pin mux\, breakpoints\, watch variables/registers\; no Christmas Ev
 e session.\n\nTHIS WEEK'S RESOURCES:\n- STM32Cube IDE: https://www.st.com/
 content/st_com/en/stm32cubeide.html\n- STM32 MCU Developer Zone: https://w
 ww.st.com/content/st_com/en/stm32-mcu-developer-zone.html\n\nMASTERY GATE:
  Build/flash a GPIO project\, use breakpoints/watch\, explain the major bu
 ild/debug steps\, and document one real debug issue.\n\nSYSTEM RULES: Watc
 hing alone never counts. Use learn -> retrieve -> calculate/code -> build/
 measure -> debug ->document. Start each non-exam study block with ~10 minu
 tes of spaced recall. Never use a missed session as justification for an u
 nhealthy make-up marathon.\n\nTODAY — ACTION CHECKLIST:\n1. Open LAB-016
  — STM32 GPIO + SWD Bring-Up in Practice → Lab Center → Week 16.\n2.
  Choose one route. Virtual needs: Web browser only. Physical needs: STM32 
 board\, ST-LINK/SWD\, CubeIDE/CubeMX\, DMM/scope.\n3. Follow every numbere
 d step under the route you selected\; predict the result before simulating
 \, building\, or measuring.\n4. Save the required evidence: Wokwi project 
 link/screenshot + pinout + debug/log evidence + bring-up fault note.\n5. M
 ark only the route you actually completed. A virtual completion does not c
 laim physical bench experience.\n\nREFERENCE HUB — KEEP THESE LINKS:\n
 • Official Associate CET Study Guide\, 6th ed.: use your scanned/Google 
 Drive copy and the PRINTED page numbers in BOOK ASSIGNMENT.\n• ETA 6th-e
 d. errata: https://www.etai.org/6thEdASTErrataSheet.pdf\n\nOFFICIAL STUDY 
 GUIDE — V6 BOOK ASSIGNMENT\nREAD / USE: Targeted Ch.17 pp.149–155 revi
 ew while setting up STM32.\nQUIZ / REVIEW: Explain MPU/memory/I/O/control 
 blocks without notes before the GPIO lab.\nWHY: The book gives architectur
 e vocabulary\; STM32 practice turns it into real MCU bring-up/debug capabi
 lity.\nSOURCE RULE: The 6th-edition book is the primary CETa textbook. Cur
 rent ETA competency wording + current ETA errata control whenever the olde
 r book differs or omits a current topic.\n\nAFTER TODAY — REQUIRED LEARN
 ING OUTCOMES (WEEK 16)\nBy the end of this event\, you should be able to e
 xplain or demonstrate:\n• Explain what an MCU and ARM Cortex-M core are 
 and identify flash\, RAM\, GPIO\, clocks\, and peripherals in an STM32 sys
 tem.\n• Create/configure a basic STM32 project in STM32Cube IDE/CubeMX.\
 n• Configure GPIO input/output and understand pin multiplexing at a work
 ing level.\n• Build\, flash\, run\, and reset firmware on a real board.\
 n• Use SWD debugging: breakpoints\, single-step\, watch variables\, and 
 basic register inspection.\n• Separate a firmware problem from a board/p
 ower/configuration problem during bring-up.\n\nCOVERAGE AUDIT\nThe assigne
 d resources and tasks above are intended to cover every item in this outco
 me list. If you still cannot explain or demonstrate one of these items aft
 er the session\, do not count that item as complete. Write the concept on 
 a short catch-up list and use the linked official or supplemental resource
  before closing the gap.\n\nWEEK-END MASTERY RULE\nThis practical is the c
 umulative proof point for the week. The week is Green only when the knowle
 dge can be used on real hardware/code/measurements without following a ste
 p-by-step tutorial.\n\nSIMPLE COMPLETION RULE\nFinish the assigned work\, 
 then check the learning outcomes below. If you cannot explain or demonstra
 te an outcome\, write that one concept on a short catch-up list and repair
  it during the next available review block. No spreadsheet logging is requ
 ired.
