SUMMARY:W27 • Learn — Python Hardware Testing & Data Logging
DESCRIPTION:ALFRED UNIVERSITY | AU-ESET 301\n\nALFRED STUDY PATH — OPEN T
 HESE WHEN YOU NEED THEM\n• Week module: https://numberclaw2.github.io/al
 fred-university/week.html?week=27\n• Search this topic: https://numbercl
 aw2.github.io/alfred-university/search.html?q=Python%20Hardware%20Test%20%
 2B%20Automated%20Logger\n• Engineering Library: https://numberclaw2.gith
 ub.io/alfred-university/resources.html\nCAREER CONNECTION: Portfolio-grade
  hardware/firmware/test evidence.\nRESOURCE PRIORITY: CETa Core → Career
  Core → Deep Dive. Complete the required work first\; use Deep Dive for 
 repair or extra depth.\n\nWEEK 27 FOCUS: Python Hardware Test + Automated 
 Logger\nETA/CETa SCOPE: 21.4/21.7 reinforcement\nCAREER/EMBEDDED PURPOSE: 
 pySerial\, CSV/JSON\, timestamps\, pytest-style PASS/FAIL\, plots\, malfor
 med-response handling and repeatable test steps.\n\nTHIS WEEK'S RESOURCES:
 \n- pySerial docs: https://pyserial.readthedocs.io/\n-pytest getting start
 ed: https://docs.pytest.org/en/stable/getting-started.html\n- Matplotlib Q
 uick start: https://matplotlib.org/stable/users/explain/quick_start.html\n
 \nMASTERY GATE: Host tool can command hardware\, log results and automatic
 ally determine PASS/FAIL for at least three requirements.\n\nSYSTEM RULES:
  Watching alone never counts. Use learn ->retrieve -> calculate/code -> bu
 ild/measure -> debug -> document. start each non-exam study block with ~10
  minutes of spaced recall. Never use a missed session as justification for
  an unhealthy make-up marathon.\n\nTODAY:\nFirst 10 min: spaced recall fro
 m prior weeks. Study this week’s scoped material and make a one-page mem
 ory summary. Complete8–12 fresh CETa questions when ETA content applies.
  School work takes priority during exam-heavy weeks.\n\nPYTHON TEST REFERE
 NCES:\n• pySerial: https://pyserial.readthedocs.io/\n• pytest: https:/
 /docs.pytest.org/en/stable/\n• Matplotlib Quick Start: https://matplotli
 b.org/stable/users/explain/quick_start.html\n\nREFERENCE HUB — KEEP THES
 E LINKS:\n• Official Associate CET Study Guide\, 6th ed.: use your scann
 ed/Google Drive copy and the PRINTED page numbers in BOOK ASSIGNMENT.\n•
  ETA 6th-ed. errata: https://www.etai.org/6thEdASTErrataSheet.pdf\n\nOFFIC
 IAL STUDY GUIDE — V6 BOOK ASSIGNMENT\nREAD / USE: Reference Ch.22 pp.188
 –193 while writing Python test/logger documentation.\nQUIZ / REVIEW: Use
  the Ch.22 quiz p.193 only if your technical-writing fundamentals are weak
 .\nWHY: Makes the automated-test artifact readable and professional\, not 
 just functional.\nSOURCE RULE: The 6th-edition book is the primary CETa te
 xtbook. Current ETA competency wording + current ETA errata control whenev
 er the older book differs or omits a current topic.\n\nAFTER TODAY — REQ
 UIRED LEARNING OUTCOMES (WEEK 27)\nBy the end of this event\, you should b
 e able to explain or demonstrate:\n• Build a Python host/test tool that 
 communicates with the DUT over serial.\n• Define commands/responses and 
 parse them robustly.\n• Log timestamped results to CSV/JSON or another s
 tructured format.\n• Turn requirements into repeatable PASS/FAIL checks 
 using assertions/pytest-style logic.\n\nCOVERAGE AUDIT\nThe assigned resou
 rces and tasks above are intended to cover every item in this outcome list
 . If you still cannot explain or demonstrate one of these items after the 
 session\, do not count that item as complete. Write the concept on a short
  catch-up list and use the linked official or supplemental resource before
  closing the gap.\n\nSIMPLE COMPLETION RULE\nFinish the assigned work\, th
 en check the learning outcomes below. If you cannot explain or demonstrate
  an outcome\, write that one concept on a short catch-up list and repair i
 t during the next available review block. No spreadsheet logging is requir
 ed.
STATUS:CONFIRMED
TRANSP:OPAQUE
CATEGORIES:Embedded\,CETa
