 ithub.io/alfred-university/labs.html\n• Engineering Library: https://num
 berclaw2.github.io/alfred-university/resources.html\nCAREER CONNECTION: Po
 rtfolio-grade hardware/firmware/test evidence.\nRESOURCE PRIORITY: CETa Co
 re → Career Core → Deep Dive. Complete the required work first\; use D
 eep Dive for repair or extra depth.\n\nWEEK 28 FOCUS: Fault Injection + Tr
 oubleshooting + Test Report\nETA/CETa SCOPE: 6.x/8.x/21.4/21.7 spaced revi
 ew\nCAREER/EMBEDDED PURPOSE: Seed sensor disconnect\, wrong address\, bad 
 wire\, timeout\, config error\, power fault and firmware defect\; use one-
 change-at-a-time diagnosis.\n\nTHIS WEEK'S RESOURCES:\n- ETA CETa competen
 cies: https://www.etai.org/comps/CETa_comps.pdf\n\nMASTERY GATE: At least 
 three interview-ready failure stories\, including onephysical/electrical f
 ault\, each with reproducible→measure→isolate→fix→verify evidence.
 \n\nSYSTEM RULES: watching alone never counts. Use learn -> retrieve -> ca
 lculate/code ->build/measure -> debug -> document. Start each non-exam stu
 dy block with~10 minutes of spaced recall. Never use a missed session as j
 ustification for an unhealthy make-up marathon.\n\nTODAY — ACTION CHECKL
 IST:\n1. Write at least three deliberate faults before testing: one hardwa
 re/power/wiring fault\, one firmware/configuration fault\, and one interfa
 ce/test-fixture fault.\n2. For each fault\, record the expected symptom\, 
 reproduce it safely\, and collect DMM/scope/logic/serial evidence.\n3. Fol
 low reproduce → measure → isolate → hypothesize → test → fix →
  retest\; do not change multiple variables at once.\n4. Run the original p
 assing test again after each repair to check for regression.\n5. Save a te
 chnical report containing test setup\, fault matrix\, observations\, root 
 cause\, corrective action\, and final PASS/FAIL evidence.\n\nREFERENCE HUB
  — KEEP THESE LINKS:\n• Official Associate CET Study Guide\, 6th ed.: 
 use your scanned/Google Drive copy and the PRINTED page numbers in BOOK AS
 SIGNMENT.\n• ETA 6th-ed. errata: https://www.etai.org/6thEdASTErrataShee
 t.pdf\n\nOFFICIAL STUDY GUIDE — V6 BOOK ASSIGNMENT\nREAD / USE: Referenc
 e Ch.18 pp.159–164 + Ch.22 pp.188–193 + Appendix C pp.217–218.\nQUIZ
  / REVIEW: Use troubleshooting/technical-writing questions as retrieval\; 
 focus on evidence quality.\nWHY: Fault injection and test reports are wher
 e CETa troubleshooting turns into employer-ready proof.\nSOURCE RULE: The 
 6th-edition book is the primary CETa textbook. Current ETA competency word
 ing + current ETA errata control whenever the older book differs or omits 
 a current topic.\n\nAFTER TODAY — REQUIRED LEARNING OUTCOMES (WEEK 28)\n
 By the end of this event\, you should be able to explain or demonstrate:\n
 • Design and execute deliberate fault-injection tests.\n• Use the trou
 bleshooting sequence: reproduce\, measure\, isolate\, hypothesize\, test\,
  fix\, and verify.\n• Distinguish hardware\, firmware\, interface\, test
 -fixture\, power\, and host-script faults.\n• Collect DMM/scope/logic-an
 alyzer/log evidence that supports root cause.\n\nCOVERAGE AUDIT\nThe assig
 ned resources and tasks above are intended to cover every item in this out
 come list. If you still cannot explain or demonstrate one of these items a
 fter the session\, do not count that item as complete. Write the concept o
 n a short catch-up list and use the linked official or supplemental resour
 ce before closing the gap.\n\nSIMPLE COMPLETION RULE\nFinish the assigned 
 work\, then check the learning outcomes below. If you cannot explain or de
 monstrate an outcome\, write that one concept on a short catch-up list and
  repair it during the next available review block. No spreadsheet logging 
 is required.
